# aa-counter

aa-counter is the sample tool that `cli generate example` writes. It counts
how often one amino acid occurs in each peptide of a list. The commands below
run its predict, preprocess, and postprocess subcommands end to end.

## Set up

```bash
cli generate example
cd aa-counter
uv sync
```

`cli generate example` always names the folder `aa-counter/`. Run every
command below from that folder. The commands write to `../aa-counter-out/`,
outside the project, because `make build` packs the files in the project
folder into the tarball.

## Predict

`predict` reads one input file: JSON with `-j` or TSV with `-t`. The JSON file
names the amino acid to count. With a TSV file, `-a` names it.

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j src/example.json
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -t src/example.tsv -a P
```

Without `-o`, the result prints to the screen as TSV. The first command prints:

```text
peptide	length	AAcount(L)
ADMGHLKY	8	1
ELDDTLKY	8	2
FMDHVLRYP	9	1
...
```

`-f json` prints JSON instead. `-o` takes an output prefix and creates its
folder: `-o ../aa-counter-out/result` writes `../aa-counter-out/result.tsv`.

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j src/example.json -f json
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j src/example.json -o ../aa-counter-out/result
```

## Preprocess

`preprocess` splits one JSON input into jobs and lists them in
`job_descriptions.json`. aa-counter makes one job per peptide length. The
command creates the `-o` folder and the folders inside it.

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py preprocess -j src/example.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs
```

```text
../aa-counter-out/jobs/
├── job_descriptions.json   4 jobs: 3 predict jobs, then 1 postprocess job
├── predict-inputs/
│   ├── data/               the peptides of each job, one file per job
│   └── params/             the parameters of each job, one JSON file per job
├── predict-outputs/        empty until the predict jobs run
├── aggregate/              empty until the postprocess job runs
└── results/                empty; postprocess -p uses it below
```

The example peptides have 3 lengths (8, 9, and 10), so there are 3 predict
jobs. The last entry is the postprocess job, and `depends_on_job_ids` lists
the jobs that must finish first:

```json
{
    "shell_cmd": "/path/to/aa-counter/src/run_aa_counter.py postprocess --job-desc-file=../aa-counter-out/jobs/job_descriptions.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs/aggregate/final-result -f json",
    "job_id": 3,
    "job_type": "postprocess",
    "depends_on_job_ids": [
        0,
        1,
        2
    ],
    "expected_outputs": [
        "../aa-counter-out/jobs/aggregate/final-result.json"
    ]
}
```

## Run the jobs

Each `shell_cmd` starts with the path to `src/run_aa_counter.py`. The script
is not executable, so run each command with `uv run python` in front. The
first line below writes the commands to `jobs.txt`, and the second runs them
in order:

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python -c 'import json; print("\n".join(j["shell_cmd"] for j in json.load(open("../aa-counter-out/jobs/job_descriptions.json"))))' > ../aa-counter-out/jobs/jobs.txt
xargs -L1 uv run python < ../aa-counter-out/jobs/jobs.txt
```

`jobs.txt` holds one command per job. Each parameter file name ends in a
random suffix:

```text
/path/to/aa-counter/src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-inputs/params/0-tyitmshd.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-outputs/result.0 -f json
/path/to/aa-counter/src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-inputs/params/1-dbafrisq.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-outputs/result.1 -f json
/path/to/aa-counter/src/run_aa_counter.py predict -j ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-inputs/params/2-zra1ewrh.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-outputs/result.2 -f json
/path/to/aa-counter/src/run_aa_counter.py postprocess --job-desc-file=../aa-counter-out/jobs/job_descriptions.json -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs/aggregate/final-result -f json
```

The preprocess `-o` was relative, so these paths are too. Run the jobs from
the project folder.

## Postprocess

To merge the results again by hand, pass the job descriptions file.
Without `-o`, postprocess writes the output that the postprocess job lists:

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py postprocess --job-desc-file ../aa-counter-out/jobs/job_descriptions.json
```

`--input-results-dir` merges every result file in a folder instead. With it,
give `-o <prefix>` to write `<prefix>.json`, or `-p`, an existing folder to
write `final-result.json` to. With neither, aa-counter stops with a usage error.
With `--job-desc-file`, aa-counter does not write to `-p`, but a `-p` you pass
must still be an existing folder.

<!-- run -->
```bash
uv run python src/run_aa_counter.py postprocess --input-results-dir ../aa-counter-out/jobs/predict-outputs -p ../aa-counter-out/jobs/results
```

postprocess takes exactly one of `--job-desc-file` and `--input-results-dir`.
It writes JSON by default, and `-f tsv` writes TSV.

## Output locations

| Command | Writes |
|---|---|
| `predict -o ../aa-counter-out/result` | `../aa-counter-out/result.tsv` |
| `preprocess -o ../aa-counter-out/jobs` | `jobs/job_descriptions.json`, `jobs/predict-inputs/` |
| each predict job | `jobs/predict-outputs/result.0.json` to `result.2.json` |
| the postprocess job, or `postprocess --job-desc-file` | `jobs/aggregate/final-result.json` |
| `postprocess --input-results-dir ... -p jobs/results` | `jobs/results/final-result.json` |

## Code to read next

| File | What it shows |
|---|---|
| `src/AACounterArgumentParser.py` | The tool description and the `predict` options |
| `src/run_aa_counter.py` | The `predict` branch: read the input, count, write the result |
| `src/preprocess.py` | Splitting the input by length and writing `job_descriptions.json` |
| `src/postprocess.py` | Merging the job results into one result |
